# The market recorder for the replay harness. Read by scripts/gen_launchd.py.

[job]
name = recorder
exit_timeout = 300
run_at_load = false

[program]
{VENV_PYTHON}
{ROOT}/agent/replay/record_day.py
--once

[environment]
TZ = America/New_York
PATH = /usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/opt/homebrew/bin
AGENTIC_TRADING_ROOT = {ROOT}

[schedule]
every 5 minutes from 09:25 to 16:05 on weekdays

[comment]
The market recorder, run by launchd.

It wakes {ROOT}/agent/replay/record_day.py with the once flag every five minutes
from 09:25 to 16:05, Monday to Friday. That is 81 wake ups a day and 405 entries
below.

WHY THIS EXISTS. Before any of the five books is allowed to place a real paper
order, the whole loop has to be replayed against a recorded trading day with a
fake broker, and every guardrail has to be seen to fire. This job records the
day. What it records, and what the replay then proves, is written up in
{ROOT}/docs/REPLAY.md.

It is meant for one day at a time, which is why the weekday entries are here
rather than a single date: launchd has no way to say "just this one Tuesday", so
it is loaded on the day and unloaded afterwards.

WHY ONE WAKE UP PER TICK RATHER THAN LETTING THE SCRIPT LOOP. record_day.py can
run its own six and a half hour loop, and for a person watching a terminal that
is the easier way. Under launchd it is the wrong way: a script that loops for
six and a half hours is one crash away from losing the rest of the day, and a
Mac that sleeps at noon wakes up with a loop that thinks it is still 11:55. One
wake up per tick means a tick that dies costs one tick.

IT PLACES NO ORDERS AND CANNOT. record_day.py connects to IB Gateway read only
and calls nothing but quotes and historical bars. The 09:35 and 09:40 ticks also
run agent/scanner.py, which is read only for the same reason.
